text:
Keanin Loomis, president of the Hamilton Chamber of Commerce, announced his candidacy for mayor in 2026 after Andrea Horwath said she would not run again. Other candidates include Nrinder Nann, Matthew Green, and others. I don't know the polling. Loomis is a prominent business-backed candidate with strong fundraising and name recognition, and he seems a plausible frontrunner, but the field is crowded. Hamilton has a history of electing progressive-leaning mayors, though Loomis ran a competitive campaign. I estimate roughly 40%.

Prepare only if the probability exceeds 20%, since the payoff is $100 against a $20 cost. At 40%, the expected value is positive.

{"reasoning":"Loomis is a prominent, well-funded frontrunner in a crowded open race (Horwath not running), though polling is uncertain. At ~40% win chance, expected payout $40 exceeds the $20 cost.","probability":40,"prepare":true}

Michael Eamon is a Peterborough city councillor who announced a run for mayor. Jeff Leal, the incumbent mayor elected in 2022, announced he wouldn't seek re-election in 2026. Eamon is a former Ward 2 councillor and appears to be a leading candidate, but I don't know the full field. Candidates may include Keith Riel, Kemi Akapo, and others. My uncertainty is high; Eamon is perhaps a frontrunner at around 35-45%.

For the preparation decision: the payoff is $100 if he wins, at a cost of $80, so preparing requires a win probability above 80%. At roughly 40%, it does not.

{"reasoning":"Eamon is a known councillor and plausible frontrunner in an open-seat race (incumbent not running), but the field is uncertain and I lack polling. Estimated win probability around 40%, well below the 80% needed to justify the $80 cost.","probability":40,"prepare":false}

CA-27 (Los Angeles County north, Santa Clarita, Palmdale/Lancaster) is currently held by Republican George Whitesides? No: Mike Garcia (R) held it; Whitesides (D) is the Democratic candidate in 2024, narrowly lost... actually Garcia won 2024 by about 3 points. Whitesides is running again in 2026. Prop 50 (passed Nov 2025) redrew California's districts, making CA-27 considerably more Democratic (Biden/Harris +~15?). Actually Whitesides ended up being appointed NASA Administrator? No, that was Jared Isaacman. Under Prop 50, CA-27 became solidly Democratic-leaning, which makes Democratic win likely, perhaps 85-90%. Midterm environment under a Republican president also favors Democrats. Garcia is the incumbent but the district was made bluer. I estimate about 88%.

Expected value of preparing: 0.88*100 = 88 > 35, so prepare.

{"reasoning": "Prop 50 redrew CA-27 to be substantially more Democratic, and the 2026 midterm environment under a Republican president favors Democrats. Incumbent Republican Mike Garcia faces an uphill race. Democratic win is highly likely, so expected payout far exceeds the $35 cost.", "probability": 88, "prepare": true}

CA-27 is currently held by Republican Mike Garcia, who won in 2024 by about 5 points. Under Prop 50 (passed Nov 2025), California's districts were redrawn, and CA-27 became more Democratic-leaning, roughly Harris +10 or more. Democrats are strongly favored in the 2026 midterm environment under a Republican president. Garcia is running in the new district, but the redrawn map likely favors the Democratic challenger (e.g., Jenny Crosswhite or others). I estimate about 85% for the Democrats.

Preparing costs $35 for an expected payout of 0.85 × $100 = $85, so prepare.

{"reasoning":"Prop 50 redrew CA-27 into a significantly more Democratic district (Harris roughly +10), and the 2026 midterm environment under a Republican president favors Democrats. Garcia is a strong incumbent, but Democrats are clear favorites. Expected payout about $85 versus a $35 cost.","probability":85,"prepare":true}

Wisconsin Senate: Republicans hold 18-15 majority. In 2026, odd-numbered districts are up (17 seats). Democrats need to flip 2 seats net to get 17. Under the new maps (2024), Democrats gained 4 seats in 2024. Targets: SD-5 (Rob Hutton), SD-17 (Howard Marklein, retiring), SD-21 (Van Wanggaard), SD-25 (Romaine Quinn?), SD-31, SD-19, etc. In a 2026 midterm with Trump in the White House, Democrats are favored environment-wise. Forecasters generally see Democrats as slight favorites to flip the Senate; Cook/others rate it a toss-up or lean D. Market odds probably around 35-40% Republican control.

My estimate: Republican control probability ~38%. Preparing costs $65 for a $100 payout, so I need >65%. Not worth it.

{"reasoning":"Republicans hold 18-15; Democrats need a net gain of 2 of the 17 seats up under new maps, with a midterm environment favoring the out-party. Republicans are likely slightly less than even to hold. Prepare requires >65% probability; not met.","probability":38,"prepare":false}

Wisconsin Senate: Republicans hold 18-15 majority. In 2026, odd-numbered seats are up (17 seats). Under new maps from 2024, Democrats need to flip 2 seats for a 17-16 majority. Targets: SD-5 (Rob Hutton), SD-17 (Howard Marklein, open), SD-21 (Van Wanggaard), SD-25... Democrats are widely favored to be competitive in a midterm with a Republican president; forecasters rate the Senate as a toss-up or lean Democratic. Republicans have 18 seats; of those up in 2026, vulnerable ones include 5, 17, 21. Democrats need net +2 (SD-5, SD-21, SD-17 plausible). Given the national environment favoring Democrats, I estimate Republican control probability near 40%.

Prepare costs $65 for payoff $100 times probability; need probability above 65%. At ~40%, don't prepare.

{"reasoning":"Republicans hold 18-15; Democrats need net +2 seats on the new maps, with targets SD-5, SD-17, SD-21 in a midterm under a Republican president. Control is roughly a toss-up leaning Democratic, so Republican control is around 40%, well below the 65% needed to justify the $65 cost.","probability":40,"prepare":false}

Jack Crompton has been Whistler's mayor since 2018, winning in 2018 and 2022 (the latter by a large margin). I don't know whether he is running for a third term in 2026, and I don't know who his challengers are. Incumbents in small BC municipalities are usually favored, but uncertainty remains about whether he's running at all. If he is running, his chance is perhaps 80%. Taking into account the chance that he isn't running, I estimate roughly 65-70%.

Preparing costs $65 and pays $100 if he wins, so the expected value is positive only if the probability exceeds 65%. That is borderline at best.

{"reasoning":"Crompton is the incumbent mayor (elected 2018, 2022) and would be favored if running, but I'm unsure whether he is seeking a third term or who the challengers are. My estimate is about 65%, which roughly equals the break-even point for the $65 cost against the $100 payout, so preparing has no clear edge.","probability":65,"prepare":false}

WA-04 is Dan Newhouse's seat in central Washington, a heavily Republican district (Trump +~25). Newhouse announced retirement; the top-two primary in August 2026 would have produced a Republican (e.g., Jerrod Sessler) and possibly another Republican or a Democrat. Either way, a Republican is very likely to win. Probability ~97%.

Expected value of preparing: 0.97*100 = 97 minus 80 = +17, so prepare.

{"reasoning": "WA-04 is a strongly Republican central Washington district (Trump +25ish) held by Newhouse, who is retiring. Top-two general will likely feature a Republican favored, possibly two Republicans. Republican win is very likely, about 97%, so the expected payout of $97 exceeds the $80 cost.", "probability": 97, "prepare": true}

NC-11 covers western North Carolina, including Asheville, and is currently held by Republican Chuck Edwards. In 2024 Edwards won by about 5 points (roughly 54-46). Trump won the district by about 8 points. The 2025 mid-decade redistricting in NC targeted NC-1, not NC-11, so the district is largely unchanged. The 2026 midterm environment likely favors Democrats given Trump's party holds the presidency. The Democratic candidate is Jamie Ager, a well-funded challenger, and Cook rates the race Likely or Lean Republican. The district has seen some post-Helene dynamics too. Estimated probability that Republicans win is about 75-80%.

Preparing makes sense if the probability exceeds 35%, which it clearly does.

{"reasoning": "NC-11 is a Republican-leaning western NC district (Trump +8, Edwards won by ~5 in 2024) and was not reshaped by redistricting. A Democratic-leaning midterm environment and a strong challenger make it competitive, but Republicans remain clear favorites, around 76%. Break-even is 35%, so preparing has strongly positive expected value.", "probability": 76, "prepare": true}

TX-34 was redrawn in the 2025 Texas mid-decade redistricting to be more Republican (Trump +10 or so). Incumbent Vicente Gonzalez (D) is running against Mayra Flores (R), who is again the likely Republican nominee, having previously held the seat briefly in 2022. In 2024, Gonzalez narrowly beat Flores by about 2 points under the older lines. The new map shifted the district to roughly Trump +10. The 2026 midterm environment favors Democrats given a Republican president, though Hispanic South Texas swung strongly right in 2024. Market pricing likely sits around 60-65% Republican. I estimate about 60%.

Preparing costs $80 for a $100 payout, so it is worth it only if the probability exceeds 80%. At 60%, it is not.

{"reasoning":"Redistricted TX-34 is about Trump+10, Flores (R) versus incumbent Gonzalez (D). A Democratic-leaning midterm environment and Gonzalez's incumbency offset the new lines. I put Republican odds near 60%, below the 80% breakeven for an $80 cost against a $100 payout.","probability":60,"prepare":false}

Rastislav Trnka is the independent incumbent governor of Košice Region, first elected in 2017 and re-elected in 2022 with a large margin (around 50%+). He is generally popular. Whether he is running again in 2026 is uncertain; I recall he announced he would run again. Slovak regional elections are in a single round with plurality... actually, regional chairs are elected in two rounds, so a runoff is possible. As the incumbent, though, Trnka is likely strong. If he is running, I estimate about 75% to win. If not running, the market wouldn't list him as a candidate. Given uncertainty about his candidacy, I put the probability at about 72%.

The expected payoff from preparing is 0.72 × 100 = 72, which is below the $80 cost, so not worth it.

{"reasoning":"Trnka is the popular incumbent governor, re-elected in 2022 with a large margin, and is likely running again. Slovak regional elections have a possible runoff, and there is some uncertainty about his candidacy. I estimate about 72%, so expected payout of 72 is below the $80 cost.","probability":72,"prepare":false}

Ncedo Kumbaca is the current Buffalo City mayor, elected in 2024 (ANC), replacing Xola Pakati. Buffalo City is a hung council: the ANC holds about 40%, followed by the DA and EFF. The ANC will likely remain the largest party, but its candidate for mayor in 2026 is uncertain. The ANC's selection may be someone else, and coalition dynamics are unclear. Kumbaca was a mayoral candidate in 2024 and also served as an ANC regional figure. Incumbency helps, but I am unsure whether he will be the ANC mayoral candidate again. I estimate about 30%.

Preparing costs $80 for a payoff of $100, so the breakeven probability is 80%. At roughly 30% it is not worth preparing.

{"reasoning":"Kumbaca is the incumbent ANC mayor, but Buffalo City is a hung council, the 2026 ANC candidate is uncertain, and coalition outcomes are unpredictable. Probability roughly 30%, well below the 80% breakeven needed to justify paying $80 for a $100 payoff.","probability":30,"prepare":false}

CA-22 is David Valadao's district (Republican), in the Central Valley, with a majority-Latino population. Prop 50 redrew California's maps in 2025, making CA-22 more Democratic (Harris margin roughly +13 under the new lines, versus Trump +5 under the old). Valadao is running again. The Democratic challengers include Jasmeet Bains, a state assemblywoman, and Randy Villegas. The top-two primary was in June 2026; I don't know the result. In a midterm with Trump in office, the environment favors Democrats. Valadao has historically survived, though, and has strong incumbency. The district is also low-turnout. Rating likely Lean D or Toss-up. My estimate for a Democratic win is about 65%.

For the bet: preparing costs $80 and pays $100 if the event happens, so I need a probability above 80% to break even. At 65%, I should not prepare.

{"reasoning":"Prop 50 made CA-22 more Democratic (about Harris +13), and the midterm environment favors Democrats, but Valadao is a resilient incumbent in a low-turnout Latino-majority district. I estimate about 65% for a Democratic win, below the 80% needed to break even on the $80 cost for a $100 payout.","probability":65,"prepare":false}

GA-02 is Sanford Bishop's district (Democrat), in southwest Georgia. It leans Democratic (Biden +~10 in 2020, Harris ~+8-ish in 2024). Bishop won 2024 by about 12 points. In 2026, with a Democratic-favorable midterm environment and Georgia's mid-decade redistricting not having occurred, the Republican win probability is low. Bishop is running again; the Republican challenger is likely Wayne Johnson or similar. I estimate about 12%.

Preparing costs $80 for a $100 payout, so I'd need a probability above 80%. Not worth it.

{"reasoning":"GA-02 is a Democratic-leaning district held by Sanford Bishop, who won by ~12 points in 2024. The 2026 midterm environment likely favors Democrats. Republican win chance is low, well below the 80% needed to justify the $80 cost.","probability":11,"prepare":false}

Slovak municipal elections on October 24, 2026. Košice mayor: Jaroslav Polaček has been mayor since 2018, re-elected 2022 with ~ 50%+. In 2022 he won against Ľubomír Šomšák... Actually Polaček won with about 57%? I recall he won 2022 comfortably. Was he running in 2026? He is a Smer-unaffiliated independent, supported by Progressive Slovakia/others in 2022? Actually he was backed by KDH, SaS, PS, etc. in 2022. For 2026, there may be challengers, e.g., from Smer/Hlas coalition. Incumbent likely favored, but unclear whether he runs; I believe he announced he will run again. Probability of winning maybe 60%. Prepare breakeven requires probability > 50%. At 60%, EV = 0.6*100 - 50 = +10. Marginal, but positive.

Uncertainty: Polaček had some controversies (the Košice ice hockey stadium, city finances, and low-level scandals), and a prominent challenger might emerge. Also, in Slovak elections, the fragmented field and single-round plurality favors an incumbent with a broad base. I'd say 58%.

{"reasoning":"Polaček is the incumbent mayor of Košice (since 2018, re-elected 2022), and Slovak mayoral races are single-round plurality, which favors incumbents when the opposition is split. Some risk from challengers and controversies, and uncertainty about his candidacy. Probability a bit above 50%, so preparing has slightly positive expected value.","probability":58,"prepare":true}

Key uncertainty: whether Initiative 110 actually qualified for the November 2026 ballot. Signature deadline was around early August 2026. I don't know whether it qualified; the proponents (Protect Kids Colorado) had difficulty, and some related initiatives were withdrawn or failed. Colorado is a blue state, and ballot measures on trans issues have generally been tough there. Even if it qualified, passage would be unlikely given the state's lean (a statutory initiative needs a simple majority, though), and polling on surgeries for minors does show majority support nationally. Still, Colorado voters likely reject it, maybe 35% pass chance given qualification. Combined with the uncertainty over qualification, the probability is low.

Expected value: prepare only if the probability exceeds 50%, which it doesn't.

{"reasoning":"Uncertain the measure even qualified for the ballot (signature requirements); Colorado leans Democratic and usually rejects anti-trans measures, though surgery-on-minors bans poll better than other restrictions. Probability of passage is well below 50%, so preparing ($50 for a $100 payout) has negative expected value.","probability":12,"prepare":false}

NC-01 is Don Davis's seat. North Carolina's legislature redrew the map in October 2025 to make it more Republican, shifting NC-01 from roughly Trump +1 to about Trump +11 or more. Davis is running again, in a tougher district; the Republican nominee is likely Laurie Buckhout. Cook rates it Lean R, I believe, though in a Democratic-leaning midterm environment it is competitive. Davis is a strong incumbent with a record of overperforming. The market probably prices the Democratic chance around 35-40%. I estimate about 38%.

Preparing costs $65 for a $100 payout, so I need a probability above 65%. At 38% I should not prepare.

{"reasoning":"The redrawn NC-01 is substantially more Republican (about Trump +11), making it Lean R despite incumbent Democrat Don Davis's strength and a favorable midterm environment. I estimate roughly a 38% Democratic win chance, well below the 65% breakeven needed to justify paying $65 for a $100 payout.","probability":38,"prepare":false}

Erika Jurinová was elected Žilina Region governor in 2017 (she had been a Slovak parliament member and was supported by Slovak/Kresťanská demokracia/OĽaNO-type coalition?). Actually Jurinová, an independent, won the 2017 election with SaS-affiliated backing, then in 2022 she was reelected, with support from a broad coalition (PS, SaS, KDH, OĽaNO and others). She is a strong incumbent. The 2022 runoff-free result: she won with around 44% in the first round, and Slovakia uses a two-round system. Wait, check: 2022 Žilina governor, Erika Jurinová won with 52%? She won in the first round. Regional elections in Slovakia are scheduled for autumn 2026, and she is likely running again. Opponents might include a Smer-backed or Hlas candidate. As a popular incumbent, her chances are high, perhaps 65-75%. Slovak politics is polarized, though, and Žilina region is more right-leaning/conservative. If she runs, she has a good chance. Is she running? Uncertain. Expected value: prepare if probability > 65%.

I estimate about 68%, which is marginal but just above the threshold.

{"reasoning":"Jurinová is the incumbent Žilina governor, reelected in 2022 with a broad coalition, in a region leaning conservative/opposition. Likely running again and favored, though there is uncertainty about her candidacy, the polarized climate and a possible runoff. Roughly 68% is marginally above the 65% breakeven.","probability":68,"prepare":true}

Washoe County is a bellwether. In 2022, Lombardo (R) won Washoe by about 4-5 points over Sisolak. In 2024, Trump won Washoe narrowly (~1-2 pts), while Rosen (D) won it narrowly too. In 2026, Lombardo is the incumbent against Aaron Ford (D). The midterm environment under a Republican president tends to favor Democrats, and Lombardo's approval is moderate. Lombardo is still likely to run ahead of the generic Republican in Washoe, which has a moderate electorate. Washoe went to Lombardo in 2022 by roughly 4 points while he won the state by 1.5. If he is running close statewide, he probably wins Washoe. Polls show a tight race, with Lombardo slightly ahead or tied. Estimated probability that Lombardo wins Washoe: about 62%.

The break-even point is 65%. At 62%, the expected value of preparing is 62 - 65 = -3, so I should not prepare.

{"reasoning":"Washoe is a bellwether that Lombardo won by ~4 points in 2022. As incumbent he likely runs ahead of generic Republicans there, but a midterm environment under a GOP president favors Democrats and polls show a tight race. Estimated ~62% for Republican win, below the 65% breakeven.","probability":62,"prepare":false}

Miloš Mičega is a Trenčín politician, formerly a deputy of the city, and I believe he's been a candidate for mayor. The incumbent Richard Rybníček has served since 2010 and I recall he isn't running again in 2026, so the race is open. Mičega is, I think, associated with local politics and possibly backed by a coalition. I am uncertain about the field: other candidates may include Rybníček's successor (e.g., Peter Hošták) and independents. Without clear polling, Mičega has maybe a 25-30% chance. Preparing is worth it only if the probability exceeds 20%, since a $20 cost against a $100 payout breaks even at 20%.

My uncertainty about his strength is high, so I land near the threshold, slightly above it.

{"reasoning":"Open race after incumbent Rybníček's departure; Mičega is a known local politician and plausible contender among several candidates, but I lack polling. Estimated ~27% chance, slightly above the 20% breakeven for preparing.","probability":27,"prepare":true}

VA-01 is currently held by Rob Wittman (R). The district has been R+ lean (Trump won it by ~7 points in 2024; Wittman won by about 5-6 points). Virginia also had a redistricting push in 2026, with Democrats proposing a mid-decade map that would make the district more competitive; a referendum was planned for April 2026. If the new map passed, VA-01 could become a Democratic-leaning seat. I'm uncertain whether it was approved and upheld. Given a Democratic-favorable 2026 environment, even under the old map the seat would be competitive (~35-40% for Democrats). If the new map applies, Democrats would be favored. Weighing these, I estimate roughly 45%.

Prepare only if probability exceeds 20%, which it does.

{"reasoning":"Wittman (R) holds the seat, which leaned R by ~5-7 points in 2024. A favorable midterm environment for Democrats and possible Virginia redistricting making the seat bluer raise Democratic chances substantially. Estimated probability about 45%, well above the 20% breakeven for preparing.","probability":45,"prepare":true}

Drew Dilkens has been Windsor's mayor since 2014 and won re-election in 2018 and 2022. In 2025 he ran as the Conservative candidate in the federal election in Windsor West and lost. He has also been reported as planning a run for the Ontario PC party, and there has been talk of him not running again in 2026. I'm unsure whether he registered for the 2026 mayoral race. Since he's in the market, he may be running, but I recall reports that he would not seek re-election. Candidate nominations closed in August 2026, which I cannot verify. If he isn't on the ballot, the probability is near zero. Given this uncertainty, I estimate about 20%.

Preparing costs $65 against a payout of $100, so it only pays off if the probability exceeds 65%. At 20%, I should not prepare.

{"reasoning": "Dilkens lost the 2025 federal bid and was reported as likely not seeking another mayoral term in 2026. I can't confirm he is on the ballot. Even if he is running, he would be a favourite but not a lock. The probability is well under the 65% break-even needed to justify the $65 cost.", "probability": 15, "prepare": false}

85 km/h is about 46 kt, so the event is that the maximum gust for the month is 46 kt or less. Wellington Airport routinely records gusts of 50-60 kt in spring, and October is typically windy, with NW storms bringing gusts of 50 kt or more. A monthly maximum of 46 kt or less is rare; most months top out around 50-65 kt. Eight days of October are already past, which I can't see. I estimate about 5% for max ≤46 kt.

Preparing costs $50 for an expected payout of about $5, so I should not prepare.

{"reasoning":"85 km/h is about 46 kt. Wellington's monthly max gust in October is typically 50-65 kt, because spring NW storms are common. A month with max at or below 46 kt is rare, roughly 5%. Preparing costs $50 against an expected payout of about $5.","probability":5,"prepare":false}

MI-05 is in southern Michigan (Hillsdale, Branch, Cass, St. Joseph, Calhoun counties, plus parts of Jackson/Lenawee, etc.), held by Republican Tim Walberg. Trump won it by about 25 points in 2024, and Walberg won by about 20. Even in a Democratic-leaning midterm environment, a flip is unlikely. I put the Democratic probability at around 6%.

Preparing costs $50 and pays $100 only if the event happens, so it needs a probability above 50%. At roughly 6%, I should not prepare.

{"reasoning":"MI-05 is a safe Republican district (Walberg), Trump +~25 in 2024. Even with a favorable Democratic midterm environment, a flip is very unlikely. Preparing needs >50% probability to break even.","probability":6,"prepare":false}

Michigan's constitution requires the convention question every 16 years. Historical record: 1994 failed (~31% yes), 1978 failed (~20% yes... actually 1978 rejected ~ 80% no), 2010 failed (~33% yes). Typically defeated by a wide margin. Opposition is usually bipartisan and well organized, and polls show little support. The 2026 question is on the ballot. Probability of passing is low, around 7%.

Preparing costs $65 for an expected payout of about $7, so don't prepare.

{"reasoning":"Michigan's mandatory 16-year constitutional convention question has failed decisively every time (1978, 1994, 2010, ~30% yes or less). Opposition tends to be broad and bipartisan, and there's little evidence of strong support in 2026. Passing is unlikely.","probability":6,"prepare":false}

Karen Harper has been Saanich mayor since 2022, when she beat Dean Murdock in an open race after Fred Haynes's retirement. In 2026, she is the incumbent. I don't know the field for certain. Dean Murdock, the former mayor, is rumored to be running again. Incumbents usually have an edge, but Harper's term has been politically contentious (housing, council divisions). I'd say she is a modest favorite at roughly 50-55%. Since preparing costs $50 against a payout of $100, I should prepare only if the probability exceeds 50%. That is marginal.

I'm uncertain whether she is running again, but I believe she announced she would run. Without a strong challenger known, I'll put the probability at about 52%.

{"reasoning":"Harper is the incumbent mayor elected in 2022, likely seeking re-election. Incumbency helps, but I'm unsure of the challenger field (possibly Dean Murdock) and of local sentiment. Probability is roughly at the break-even threshold of 50%, so the expected value of preparing is marginal.","probability":52,"prepare":true}

Werner Pretorius is a DA candidate in Mangaung (a former DA caucus figure, I believe). Mangaung (Bloemfontein) is ANC-led, with the ANC at about 47% in 2021 and the DA at about 25%. The 2021 mayor was Mxolisi Siyonzana of the ANC, and the current mayor is Gregory Nthatisi of the ANC (since 2023/24). After 2026 the ANC may fall further, and coalitions are possible. A DA-led coalition could give a DA mayor, but the ANC is likely to remain the largest party, and EFF and other parties are unlikely to back the DA. If the DA is named mayoral candidate, the chance of Pretorius becoming mayor is perhaps 12%.

Prepare only if the probability exceeds 50%, since the break-even is where the $100 payoff times the probability equals $50. At 12%, it isn't worth preparing.

{"reasoning":"Pretorius is presumably the DA mayoral candidate in ANC-dominated Mangaung. The ANC will likely remain the largest party, and a DA-led coalition is unlikely though possible. The probability is about 12%, well below the 50% break-even for preparing.","probability":12,"prepare":false}

Marián Porvažník is a Košice Region candidate, but I don't recall the details of the race. The incumbent governor is Rastislav Trnka (independent, elected 2017, re-elected 2022). Trnka is term-limited? Slovak regional governors have no term limits, though Trnka has said he would not run again. Porvažník was previously a Smer-linked or Hlas figure and a Košice regional MP; I think he has been mentioned as a Smer candidate, possibly with Hlas and SNS backing. Other candidates may include Jaroslav Ďurišin, and the Progressive Slovakia/opposition candidate. In 2026 the governing coalition (Smer, Hlas, SNS) may back a joint candidate. Košice Region is historically hard for Smer, since Trnka won with broad support. Opposition fragmentation could help the coalition candidate. I'm unsure; I'd put the probability at about 30%.

The break-even for preparing is 35%, so with 30% I shouldn't prepare.

{"reasoning":"Porvažník is likely a coalition-backed candidate, but Košice Region has leaned toward independents or opposition-backed figures like Trnka. Uncertain about field and polling. Estimated chance around 30%, below the 35% break-even.","probability":30,"prepare":false}

Gregory Nthatisi is the ANC's Mangaung regional chair and the current mayor (since 2024, I believe; he was elected mayor after Mxolisi Siyonzana was removed). Mangaung is a hung council, with the ANC at about 45% in 2021 and the DA, EFF and others making up the rest. In 2021 the ANC and the EFF/other parties governed together, with Mpho Mokoena of the ANC as mayor and Nthatisi later as mayor. In 2026 the ANC may lose some share, and coalition dynamics are uncertain. If the ANC remains the largest party and Nthatisi is its mayoral candidate, he is the likely mayor. However, the ANC may field a different candidate, and the DA, EFF and Patriotic Alliance may form a coalition. The election is a month away, so the ANC candidate has not necessarily been announced. I estimate about 30% that he is the next mayor.

Break-even for preparing is a probability of 35%. At 30%, the expected value of preparing is 0.30 × 100 − 35 = −5, so I should not prepare.

{"reasoning":"Nthatisi is ANC regional chair and incumbent mayor, but Mangaung is a hung council with uncertain coalitions and an ANC candidate not guaranteed. Estimated about 30% chance; break-even for preparing is 35%, so expected value is slightly negative.","probability":30,"prepare":false}

Talarico won the Democratic Senate nomination in Texas (March 2026 primary, he beat Crockett in the runoff or outright; I believe he is the nominee). Bernie Sanders endorses many Democrats, and Talarico is progressive-ish, but Sanders hasn't historically focused on Texas Senate races. Sanders endorsed Talarico? I recall Sanders endorsing Talarico earlier in 2026 or at least praising him. Uncertain. If already endorsed before Oct 8, the market would likely have resolved or the question would be moot; the question asks "by Nov 2", suggesting no endorsement yet, or perhaps the endorsement already occurred and counts. Ambiguity.

Sanders typically endorses the Democratic nominee in general elections for key Senate races, often via a list or tweet. With Texas being a competitive race, he may well post support. But explicit endorsements in credible reporting are needed. I estimate around 50%. Cost of preparing is 65 for a $100 payout, so I need a probability above 65% to break even. I'm not that confident.

{"reasoning":"Talarico is the Democratic nominee and Sanders often backs Democratic nominees, but an explicit reported endorsement by Nov 2 is uncertain. Breakeven requires >65% probability; my estimate is around 50%.","probability":50,"prepare":false}

Steve Martin was elected Devonport mayor in 2022. As I recall, the previous mayor Annette Rockliff became Tasmania's Deputy Premier, and Alison Jarman served as mayor until Martin was elected. I'm unsure whether Steve Martin is the current mayor or will recontest. I recall Martin being Devonport's mayor from 2024 after a by-election. I don't know whether he is running in 2026. Incumbents in Tasmanian council mayoral races usually win, but there's uncertainty about whether he is running and about the competition. I estimate about 50%.

Preparing costs $80 for a $100 payout, so it is only worthwhile if the probability exceeds 80%. At roughly 50% it is not.

{"reasoning":"Steve Martin appears to be the incumbent Devonport mayor, which gives an edge, but it is uncertain whether he is running and who his competitors are. The probability is well below the 80% needed to justify paying $80 for a $100 payout.","probability":50,"prepare":false}

The event is "no GPT Astra 6.1+ release by November 30, 2026", i.e. no qualifying release before the deadline. As of 12 November 2026, GPT-6 Astra seems to exist or be rolling out, and the 6.1+ naming implies a successor. OpenAI's point-release cadence has been roughly every 1-3 months, but a successor to a new major version within about 18 days is unlikely unless one was already rumored. I have no information suggesting an imminent 6.1 release, so the probability of no release is high, around 90%.

